var AQ={RL:[["Pia packed her backpack for the hike. She put in a map, two apples, and a rain coat. Her brother Sam laughed. “It is sunny,” he said. “You will not need that coat.” Pia shrugged and tucked it in anyway. At the top of the trail, dark clouds rolled in over the hills. Pia pulled out her coat just before the rain began. Sam had left his jacket at home, and he got soaked. Pia shared her map to keep his head dry, and they hurried back down the trail."],["Leo wanted to win the race more than anything. He ran fast and passed two friends. Then his shoe came untied. Leo knew he could trip, so he stopped to tie it. Two friends passed him. His face turned red, and he wanted to quit. But he took a deep breath and kept going. He ran harder than he ever had and finished third. His coach clapped for him at the finish line. “You did not give up,” she said. Leo smiled, because she was right."],["Ana and her dad built a bird house on Saturday. They cut the boards, hammered the nails, and painted it blue. Ana hung it from the oak tree in the yard and watched it every day after school. For two weeks nothing came. Ana started to think the bird house was a bad idea. Then one morning a small wren hopped inside, carrying a twig in her beak. By the end of the week, the wren had built a nest, and Ana could hear tiny chirps."]],
RI:[["A volcano is a mountain with an opening at the top. Deep in the earth, rock gets so hot that it melts. The hot melted rock is called magma. Pressure pushes the magma up through cracks in the ground. When magma flows out of the volcano, it is called lava. Lava can be as hot as 2,000 degrees Fahrenheit. As it cools, it turns into hard black rock. Over many years, layer after layer of cooled lava can build a volcano higher and higher."],["Honey bees live together in a hive, and every bee has a job. Some bees care for the eggs and the young. Some guard the door of the hive. Others fly out to collect nectar from flowers. Nectar is a sweet liquid. The bees carry it back to the hive and turn it into honey. They store the honey in wax cells to eat during the winter, when there are no flowers. A single hive can hold thousands of bees working together."],["Wind can move sand to make a dune. A dune is a hill of sand. When wind blows over a rock or a plant, it slows down, and the sand it was carrying drops behind the rock. Grain by grain, the sand piles up. Over many years, the pile grows into a large dune. Some dunes in deserts are taller than a ten-story building. Dunes do not stay in one place, because the wind keeps pushing the sand along, slowly moving the whole hill."],["An octopus is an animal with eight arms and no bones. Because its body is soft, an octopus can squeeze through a hole no bigger than its eye. This helps it hide from sharks and other hunters. An octopus can also change color to match the rocks and sand around it. If it is still in danger, it squirts a cloud of dark ink into the water and swims away while the enemy cannot see. Octopuses eat crabs, clams, and small fish."]]};
